<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Transylvania</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Transylvania is a historical region in the Carpathian basin, now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. It was an autonomous principality under Ottoman suzerainty in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries, intervening in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-deluge">Deluge</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> of 1699 transferred it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, and it later formed part of the largely rural economy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/transylvania.jpg" alt="Transylvania" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BlinxTheKitty · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Transylvania_(Local_2-3).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Transylvania is a historical region in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>, bounded on the east and south by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathian Mountains</a> and on the west by the Apuseni Mountains. Its core is the Transylvanian Plateau, 300 to 500 metres high, drained by the Mureș, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somes">Someș</a>, Criș and Olt rivers. The name first appears in a Medieval Latin document of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> in 1078 as ultra silvam, meaning beyond the forest. The German name Siebenbürgen refers to the seven cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvanian-saxons">Transylvanian Saxons</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first known people of the territory were the Agathyrsi, followed by the Celtic La Tène culture from the 4th century BCE and the Dacian tribes, who were united under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burebista">Burebista</a>. Trajan's wars created Roman Dacia in 106, and Emperor Aurelian withdrew the legions south of the Lower Danube in 275. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gepids">Gepids</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> then held the region in turn, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a> expanded into its south in the 9th century. Hungarian tribes conquered it in the late 9th century, and King Stephen I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> brought it into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> in 1002. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> colonists settled in the 12th and 13th centuries.</p><p>Historians disagree about the early population. The theory of Daco-Roman continuity holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a> lived there without interruption, while its opponents point to the lack of written, archaeological and linguistic evidence. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs">Battle of Mohács</a> in 1526 the region belonged to the Eastern Hungarian Kingdom, from which the Principality of Transylvania emerged in 1570 under the Treaty of Speyer. For most of the 16th and 17th centuries it was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, with dual Ottoman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> suzerainty. In 1848 the Hungarian government proclaimed union with Transylvania, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-compromise-of-1867">Compromise of 1867</a> reincorporated it into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The National Assembly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a> from Transylvania proclaimed union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> on 1 December 1918, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-trianon">Treaty of Trianon</a> confirmed new borders in 1920. Over a million Hungarians remained on the Romanian side, mainly in the Székely Land. Northern Transylvania returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in 1940 under the Second Vienna Award and to Romania after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Its fortified churches, the old town of Sighișoara, the Dacian fortresses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orastie-mountains">Orăștie Mountains</a> and Roșia Montană are World Heritage Sites. Bram Stoker's 1897 novel Dracula made the region a byword for vampires in popular culture.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Transylvania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…I. The title gave a common name to the composite monarchy the Habsburgs had built since the sixteenth century: the Austrian hereditary lands, the Lands of the Bohemian Crown, the Kingdom of Hungary with Croatia, the Grand Principality of <b>Transylvania</b>, and Galicia, taken in the partitions of Poland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The plains of Hungary were among the great grain-producing regions of Europe, and Galicia, Bukovina, Dalmatia, <b>Transylvania</b> and, after 1878, Bosnia-Herzegovina had predominantly rural economies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Khmelnytsky's multi-vectored diplomacy, weighing an Orthodox–Protestant bloc, a third partner's place in the Commonwealth or the Ottoman orbit, drew the Tsardom, Poland, Sweden, <b>Transylvania</b> and the Porte into the Ukrainian question.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ottoman Sultan Suleiman the Magnificent backed Zápolya, besieged Vienna in 1529 and occupied Buda in 1541, leaving Hungary divided between a Habsburg ‘Royal Hungary’ in the north and west, an Ottoman province in the centre and an autonomous Principality of <b>Transylvania</b> in the east.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Stephen then extended his rule over the semi-independent chiefs of the basin: he overthrew his maternal uncle Gyula in <b>Transylvania</b> around 1003 and later, probably in the 1020s, destroyed Ahtum, who ruled the lands along the Maros.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>At the same time much of the country was controlled by great lords, the so-called oligarchs, who ruled their regions as independent princes: Matthew Csák in the north-west, Amadeus Aba in the north-east, Ladislaus Kán in <b>Transylvania</b> and the Kőszegi family in the west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
